  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1139 AM MDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nPlenty of mid to high level clouds are streaming across the area\nat the moment as just enough moisture has managed to work\nunderneath the high pressure. Models are indicating a weak\nshortwave rotating around the high, moving across the CWA this\nafternoon and evening, which would explain the convection that \nmodels are indicating. A few lightning strikes have already been\nnoted along the Front Range and SW Wyoming so it's only a \nmatter of time before convection fires for us as well. Despite \nthe clouds, moisture does remain relatively limited today but an\nincrease will occur later this evening and for the overnight \nhours. In fact, we'll stay a little above normal for this time \nof year with a noticeable increase from Wednesday onwards. This \nis corroborated by CAMs highlighting some isolated to low end \nscattered convection through Tuesday with more widespread \nshowers and storms Wednesday onwards. Until Wednesday, look for \ngusty outflow winds as the main concern. After that, heavy rain,\nhail and gusty winds may all make a return though storm motion \nand speed will play an important role with regards to heavy \nrain. For outdoor enthusiasts, please pay attention to the \nlatest forecasts towards the end of the week. High temperatures \nwill remain about 10 degrees above more normal August values, \npossibly dropping to below normal towards the end of the week \nthanks to increased cloud cover and precip.",
